=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client data port, also Microsoft.UI-free),
  <Name>.ViewModel.cs (INotifyPropertyChanged + IDisposable state holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent view).
  Mirrors the sibling RegenEfficiencyPage / ChargingCurvePage surfaces.
- Data sources — the web page's two hooks, each ported to the generated OpenAPI client (ADR-004):
  * useSpeedProfile → GET /analytics/speed-profile [start,end] (operation get_api_v1_analytics_speed_profile):
    the PRIMARY read; its presence gates the page success/empty branch and its failure surfaces the page
    error, exactly as the web page gates on `data ?`. Parsed into SpeedProfileSummary (Model.cs
    SpeedProfileSummary.FromJson; avg_speed_mps / peak_speed_mps / optimal_speed_mps + distribution[] of
    {speed_bucket, readings}, snake_case with camelCase alias, ?? 0). The backend response map is confirmed
    in internal/api/speedprofile/handler.go (distribution / avg_speed_mps / peak_speed_mps / optimal_speed_mps).
  * useDrives → GET /drives (operation get_api_v1_drives): the SECONDARY best-effort read that feeds the
    client-side per-bucket efficiency block + the efficiency-vs-speed scatter; a transport failure degrades to
    no drives (SpeedProfileClientFeed.FetchDrivesAsync catches ApiException → empty), mirroring the web's two
    independent queries. Parsed by SpeedProfileClientFeed.ParseDrives → SpeedDrive.FromJson.
- Projection (SpeedProfileProjection.Project) is the 1:1 port of the web render body + its getEfficiency /
  bucketColor / bucketTextClass / categoryIcon / scatterData / bucketEfficiency helpers. State precedence
  mirrors the web data lifecycle (loading → error → empty → success). The backend rollup feeds the three hero
  gauges (avg/peak/optimal, web Math.round + the 55.56 / 69.44 m/s ceilings) and the speed-distribution bar
  chart; the drive list feeds the per-bucket efficiency block (place each drive in the first distribution
  bucket whose numeric bounds contain its display speed; average the SI speed + the Wh/km efficiency) and the
  efficiency-vs-speed scatter (eligible drives with a positive speed and a positive efficiency, visible only
  when more than three exist — web scatterData.length > 3). getEfficiency reproduces the web fallback chain:
  energyUsedWh/km when energy is positive, else the (startPct − endPct)·0.75·1000/km battery estimate, else null.
- Units (P1/S5): speed is SI m/s and distance SI m on the wire; every readout is converted only at the
  projection boundary via UnitConverters.SpeedFromSi + UnitLabels.Label(units.Speed) + ScalarFormatters
  (number/percent). Efficiency is Wh/km on the wire and is converted to Wh/mi at the boundary
  (SpeedProfileProjection.EfficiencyToDisplay = ·1609.344/1000 for miles) with the unit label from
  EfficiencyUnit(units). Nothing non-SI is stored or computed.
- Nav: route name SpeedProfile → path speed-profile already exists in RouteTable.cs#298
  (Page("SpeedProfile","speed-profile",TripsDriving,"Speed Profile")); the page factory is registered in
  Shell/ShellWindow.xaml.cs (SpeedProfileRegistration.RouteName → new FeatureViews.Driving.SpeedProfilePage()).
- Catalog: 19 of the 21 web i18n keys already exist verbatim in Strings/{en,ar,he}/Resources.resw
  (translation.* prefix); the two missing keys, speedProfile.distribution.aria
  ("Speed-bucket time-share distribution bar chart") and speedProfile.effVsSpeed.aria
  ("Per-drive efficiency versus speed scatter plot"), were ADDED to all three locales. No other catalog rows
  added; every visible literal resolves through ILocalizer with the web key names (resolved once in
  SpeedProfileStrings.Resolve).

